# Flying planes by plan (domains/cognition). The ground control agent sends plans, and the AGENT
# mode of the ArduPlane fork (ardupilot/patches) flies them with the executor
# (domains/cognition/executor), the same Rust code in ArduPilot and in the tests. Two drivers run
# this section:
#   domains/cognition/executor/tests/acceptance.rs   no window and no SITL: the real executor in a
#       small point-mass plane (tests/support/plane.rs), the ground's real wire format and its
#       real scripted brain. The status (AG_*) is what mode_agent.cpp would send for it.
#   domains/cognition/runner/tests/acceptance.rs     real ArduPlane SITL with the AGENT mode: a game
#       of tag by plan, then the runner's console on the first plane. Without the AGENT build
#       (.ardupilot-agent, or SIM001_BIN) its tests are "not supported yet".
# A test runs where every state and event it needs is bound; where both can run it, the SITL
# result is the one the docs show.

Section: Flying by plan
  In this section, a computer flies the plane. The [[ground control agent]] decides what each
  plane does, about once a second. It sends the decision to the plane as an [[agent plan]].
  The plane flies the plan in [[AGENT mode]], a flight mode of our ArduPlane fork. In the
  plane, the [[executor]] turns the plan into a bank, a pitch and a throttle. ArduPilot flies
  them, as in [[FBWA]].

  The [[safety envelope]] is a part of the executor. It keeps the plane inside the [[fence]],
  above the [[floor]] and below the [[ceiling]]. It keeps the plane clear of other aircraft
  and above the stall speed. A plan cannot switch it off. The plane tells the ground its plan
  and the work of the envelope once a second.

  Use this section when you write a ground control agent, or when you fly planes by plan.
  [[Sending plans]] and [[Replacing and ending a plan]] tell you how a plan gets to the plane.
  [[Flying to a point and round it]] and [[Flying after other aircraft]] tell you how the plane
  flies each plan. [[What the envelope prevents]] and [[Other aircraft and bad data]] tell you
  what the plane does not do, whatever the plan says. [[Tag by plan]] shows the ground control
  agent in a game of [[tag]].

  The pilot always wins. If the pilot moves the mode switch of the radio, the plane leaves
  AGENT mode at once.

  The tutorials use the console of the [[runner]], with real ArduPilot planes in [[SITL]]. The
  console shows only the replies and the status of the plane. The topics also give the
  distances and the heights that we measured in a simulation of the T1-S. That simulation uses
  the same executor as the plane.

Vocabulary:
  # The plane in AGENT mode, and what the ground sends it.
  State: the flight mode of the plane is AGENT | FBWA
    Set: switch the plane to {value}: type `mode {value}` in the console
    Shows: AGENT: the heartbeat of the plane gives the flight mode AGENT (custom mode 50), and the console shows `mode=AGENT`
    Shows: FBWA: the heartbeat of the plane gives the flight mode FBWA (custom mode 5), and the console shows `mode=FBWA`
  State: the reply to the plan is none | accepted | refused for the mode | refused as malformed
    Shows: accepted: the plane replies with COMMAND_ACK, result 0 (ACCEPTED). The console shows `ack` with `result=0`
    Shows: refused for the mode: the plane replies with COMMAND_ACK, result 2 (DENIED) and the reason 1. The console shows `ack` with `result=2 reason=1`
    Shows: refused as malformed: the plane replies with COMMAND_ACK, result 2 (DENIED) and the reason 2. The console shows `ack` with `result=2 reason=2`
  State: the time to live of the plan is 0..1000 s
    Shows: 600 s: the plan is good for 600 s
    Shows: 5 s: the plan is good for 5 s
  State: the start is a level pass over the arena centre | a level pass over home | a dive at the ground | a steep climb | a slow climb | a run at the fence
    Shows: a level pass over the arena centre: the plane flies north at 15 m/s, 30 m over the centre of the fence
    Shows: a level pass over home: the plane flies north at 15 m/s, 30 m over home
    Shows: a dive at the ground: the plane dives at 22 m/s and 34 degrees, 16 m over the arena centre
    Shows: a steep climb: the plane climbs at 24 m/s and 29 degrees, 56 m over the arena centre
    Shows: a slow climb: the plane climbs at 10 m/s and 23 degrees, 22 m over the arena centre
    Shows: a run at the fence: the plane flies north at 15 m/s, 30 m high and 50 m north of the arena centre
  State: the other aircraft is far away | round the arena | after your plane | across your path
    Shows: far away: no other aircraft is near
    Shows: round the arena: the other aircraft circles the arena centre at 16 m/s and 35 m, 40 m from the centre
    Shows: after your plane: the other aircraft flies straight at your plane at 15 m/s, from 30 m behind it
    Shows: across your path: the other aircraft flies across the path of your plane at your height, and it does not look out for your plane
  State: the track of the other aircraft is heard | not heard
    Set: stop the ADSB_VEHICLE messages of the other aircraft: type `silent` in the console
    Shows: not heard: the plane hears nothing more from the other aircraft
  State: the separation limit is 0..50 m
    Set: set AGENT_SEP to {value}
  State: the fence radius is 0..2000 m
    Set: set AGENT_FENCE_R to {value}

  # What the plane reports once a second (NAMED_VALUE_INT).
  State: the reported plan is 0..65535
    Shows: goes up by 1: AG_PLAN goes up by 1, to the id of the new plan (`plan=` in the console)
    Shows: stays the same: AG_PLAN does not change
    Shows: AG_PLAN is {value}, and the console shows `plan={value}`
  State: the reported primitive is none | pursue | evade | go to | orbit | band | loiter
    Shows: none: AG_PRIM is 0 (`prim=0`)
    Shows: pursue: AG_PRIM is 1, pursue (`prim=1`)
    Shows: evade: AG_PRIM is 2, evade (`prim=2`)
    Shows: go to: AG_PRIM is 3, go to (`prim=3`)
    Shows: orbit: AG_PRIM is 4, orbit (`prim=4`)
    Shows: band: AG_PRIM is 5, band (`prim=5`)
    Shows: loiter: AG_PRIM is 6, loiter (`prim=6`)
  State: the reported fallback is none | plan expired | bad input | recovery
    Shows: none: AG_FALL is 0 (`fall=0`)
    Shows: none or recovery: the console shows `fall=0`, or `fall=3` for a short recovery
    Shows: plan expired: AG_FALL is 1 (`fall=1`), and the plane circles where it is
    Shows: bad input: AG_FALL is 2 (`fall=2`), and the plane flies wings level
    Shows: recovery: AG_FALL is 3 (`fall=3`). The plane gets its airspeed or its height back before it flies the plan again
  State: the reported interventions are 0..1000000
    Shows: above 0: AG_ENV is more than 0 (`env=` in the console)
    Shows: AG_ENV is {value}
  State: the messages about a recovery are 0..1000
    Shows: above 0: the plane sends the message "Agent: fallback 3". The console counts these messages as `recoveries`
  State: the status reports in 10 seconds are 0..100
    Shows: between 9 and 11: the console shows `reports 10`, or 9 or 11: the status comes once a second
  State: the throttle of the plane is the executor's | the pilot's
    Shows: the executor's: the executor sets the throttle
    Shows: the pilot's: the throttle stick of the radio sets the throttle

  # What the tests measure of the flight. "The point" is the point of the plan (for a loiter, where
  # the plane got the plan); "the last minute" is the 60 s before the observation; the others are
  # over the whole test.
  State: the closest approach to the point is 0..5000 m
    Shows: at most 10 m: the plane flies over the point, within 10 m of it
  State: the least distance from the point in the last minute is 0..5000 m
    Shows: at least 40 m: in the last minute, the plane is always at least 40 m from the point
    Shows: at least 25 m: in the last minute, the plane is always at least 25 m from the point
  State: the greatest distance from the point in the last minute is 0..5000 m
    Shows: at most 45 m: in the last minute, the plane stays within 45 m of the point
    Shows: at most 50 m: in the last minute, the plane stays within 50 m of the point
  State: the lowest height in the last minute is 0..500 m
    Shows: at least 34 m: in the last minute, the plane is always at least 34 m above home
    Shows: at least 38 m: in the last minute, the plane is always at least 38 m above home
    Shows: at least 43 m: in the last minute, the plane is always at least 43 m above home
  State: the highest height in the last minute is 0..500 m
    Shows: at most 42 m: in the last minute, the plane is never more than 42 m above home
    Shows: at most 46 m: in the last minute, the plane is never more than 46 m above home
    Shows: at most 47 m: in the last minute, the plane is never more than 47 m above home
  State: the interventions in the last minute are 0..100000
    Shows: 0: in the last minute, AG_ENV does not go up
  State: the lowest height of the flight is 0..500 m
    Shows: above 12 m: the plane never goes down to the floor of 12 m
  State: the highest height of the flight is 0..500 m
    Shows: at most 62 m: the plane never goes more than 2 m over the ceiling of 60 m
  State: the farthest distance from the arena centre is 0..5000 m
    Shows: at most 85 m: the plane never goes out of the fence, 85 m round the arena centre
  State: the closest distance to the other aircraft is 0..5000 m
    Shows: above 4 m: the other aircraft never comes within 4 m, across or above and below
  State: the distance to the other aircraft is 0..5000 m
    Shows: at least 35 m: the other aircraft is now at least 35 m away
  State: the greatest distance from the other aircraft in the last minute is 0..5000 m
    Shows: at most 25 m: in the last minute, the plane stays within 25 m of the other aircraft
  State: the airspeed of the plane is 0..60 m/s
    Shows: at least 14 m/s: the airspeed is back to at least 14 m/s
  State: the closest distance between two planes is 0..5000 m
    Shows: above 8 m: no two of the three planes come within 8 m, across or above and below

  # Plans, as the runner's console sends them (`plan PRIMITIVE NORTH EAST ALT RADIUS TTL [TARGET]`,
  # in metres from home). Each one has a new plan id.
  Event: the ground sends a go-to plan
    Say: type `plan goto 80 10 40 0 600` in the console. This plan tells the plane to fly to the point 80 m north and 10 m east of home, at 40 m
  Event: the ground sends a short go-to plan
    Say: type `plan goto 80 10 40 0 5` in the console. This go-to plan is good for only 5 s
  Event: the ground sends an orbit plan
    Say: type `plan orbit 70 0 45 45 600` in the console. This plan tells the plane to circle the arena centre at 45 m from it, at 45 m
  Event: the ground sends a band plan
    Say: type `plan band 70 0 35 10 600` in the console. This plan tells the plane to stay between 35 m and 45 m over the arena
  Event: the ground sends a loiter plan
    Say: type `plan loiter 0 0 0 0 600` in the console. This plan tells the plane to circle where it is
  Event: the ground sends a pursue plan
    Say: type `plan pursue 0 0 0 0 600 2` in the console. The plane chases Merlin, the aircraft with the id 2
  Event: the ground sends an evade plan
    Say: type `plan evade 0 0 0 0 600 2` in the console. The plane breaks away from Merlin, id 2
  Event: the ground sends a malformed plan
    Say: type `plan 9 80 10 40 0 600` in the console. There is no primitive 9
  Event: the ground sends the plan id again with an orbit
    Say: type `again orbit 70 0 45 45 600` in the console. This sends an orbit plan with the id of the last plan
  Event: the operator watches the status for 10 seconds
    Say: type `watch 10` in the console. The console counts the AG_PLAN messages in the next 10 s of simulated time
  Event: the plane loses its position estimate
  Event: the plane gets its position estimate back
  Event: the plane flies on for 1 second
    Say: type `wait 1` in the console
  Event: the plane flies on for 5 seconds
    Say: type `wait 5` in the console
  Event: the plane flies on for 10 seconds
    Say: type `wait 10` in the console
  Event: the plane flies on for 30 seconds
    Say: type `wait 30` in the console
  Event: the plane flies on for 2 minutes
    Say: type `wait 120` in the console
  Event: the plane flies on for 10 minutes
    Say: type `wait 600` in the console
  Event: three planes fly random plans for 20 minutes
    Say: simulate three planes on random plans for 20 minutes

  Phrase: the agent plane flies in AGENT with no plan
    Say: from the top folder of the repository, build the AGENT fork with `AP_DIR=.ardupilot-agent AGENT_EXEC=1 ardupilot/build_native.sh`. Start the runner with `SIM001_BIN=.ardupilot-agent/build/sitl/bin cargo run --release -p cognition_runner -- --agent --console --speedup 10`. Three planes take off and play a game of tag. After about 25 s, the console says `console ready`. Type `reset`. The first plane, Kestrel, flies in AGENT mode with no plan. After each command, the console shows a `status` line. After a plan, it shows an `ack` line first
    Given the flight mode of the plane is AGENT
    And the time to live of the plan is 0 s
    And the reply to the plan is none
    And the reported plan is 0
    And the reported primitive is none

Background:
  Given the agent plane flies in AGENT with no plan
